Abstract
Biological metal-organic frameworks (BioMOFs), an emerging sub-class of MOFs, are prepared from metals and biological ligands (bioligands). Benefit from the low toxicity and good biocompatibility of bioligands, BioMOFs can be used in biomedicine and biocatalysis. In this work, a novel approach was developed for fabricating BioMOFs materials (Co-Cys BioMOFs) from cobalt salt and cystine, meanwhile nitrile hydratase (NHase) was in-situ encapsulated during the synthesis process. The obtained NHase-BioMOFs biocomposits named NHase $@$ Co-Cys was characterized by SEM, TEM, XPS, etc. The preparation parameters and stabilities of NHase@Co-Cys were investigated. The maximum encapsulation yield and specific activity of NHase@Co-Cys were $92.71\%$ and $139.04~\mathrm{U/g_{i}}$ mmobilized NHase, respectively. The thermal stability of NHase@Co-Cys was improved by approximately 5-fold at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The activity of NHase after immobilization was retained nearly $60\%$ after incubating at $\mathrm{pH}~4.0$ and 10.0 for $^\mathrm{~7~h~}$ . The NHase@Co-Cys showed similar catalytic capacity compared with free NHase in producing nicotinamide. After $^\mathrm{~7~h~}$ of reaction catalyzed by free NHase (14.51 U) and NHase $@$ Co-Cys (12.76 U), the yield of nicotinamide was $90.94\%$ and $86.36\%$ , respectively. The activity of NHase $@$ Co-Cys remained $83.85\%$ of the original activity after recycling for 10 times. These results suggested that the NHase $@$ Co-Cys is an effective approach to enhance the enzymatic properties and demonstrated a broad application prospect in industrial production.
Keywords: Co-Cys BioMOFs; Immobilized enzyme; NHase; Enzymatic properties; Nicotinamide
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed of metal ions or clusters and organic ligands binding through coordination bonds, are attractive class of crystalline coordination based materials [1-4]. Benefiting from diverse choices of metal ions or clusters and organic ligands, as well as various construction strategies, MOFs have exhibited unique structural designability and multiformity [5]. Due to their tunable structures, flexible tailorability, large surface area and high porosity, MOFs exhibit great prospect for various fields like gas storage, chemical sensing, catalysis, etc[6-9]. Biologically derived MOFs (BioMOFs), an emerging sub-class of MOFs, are constructed from a variety of metals and biological ligands (bioligands) such as amino acids, peptides, nucleobases, saccharides and so on [10, 11]. Compared with other traditional organic ligands for MOFs construction, bioligands contain abundant functional groups that could supply multiple coordination sites [12]. Because of the low steric hindrance, low toxicity and good biocompatibility of bioligands, BioMOFs is widely used in biomedicine and biocatalysis [13-17]. Amino acids are a kind of ideal organic bioligands containing both amino (-NH2) and carboxyl (-COOH) groups for the construction of BioMOFs. Because of the rich coordination chemistry (through the carboxylate, amino groups and side chains), amino acids are attractive organic ligands for the preparation of MOF structures [18-19].
Enzyme catalysis has gained much attention as a green, high efficiency and sustainable catalyst, which has been applied in many processes [20, 21]. However, the widespread utilization of natural enzymes is thwarted by several drawbacks such as the instability of enzymes in harsh operational conditions, high costs and difficulties in recovery and reuse [22]. Enzyme immobilization is always considered as the most vital and practical method to overcome these drawbacks and can afford stable, recyclable biocatalysts with high retention of activity for industrial process [23-29]. Therefore, many immobilization strategies including encapsulation, carrier-free cross-linking, adsorption and covalent multipoint attachment have been developed for enzymes immobilization [30]. MOFs, especially BioMOFs, are the latest research contents in support materials. Thanks to the extremely high surface area, abundant structure constructions and biocompatibility, BioMOFs become promising alternative candidates in various biocatalytic applications [12, 31].
When MOFs are used as supports for enzyme immobilization, their unique structure and abundant supramolecular chemistry provide positive microenvironments and abundant binding sites through confinement effect and/or specific host-guest interactions [32]. What’s more, the stable structures of MOFs have enough mechanical strength, resistance of chemical attack and antimicrobial decomposition [33, 34]. Therefore, the spatial structure of enzyme can be protected from extreme environment, and the stability of the enzyme can be further improved. Typically, the synthetic approaches for immobilizing enzymes onto MOFs have been realized through three strategies: surface immobilization, diffusion into the pores of MOFs, and in-situ encapsulation [35]. Surface immobilization strategies can be divided into physical adsorption and covalent bonding [36, 37]. The method of diffusing enzymes into the pores of MOFs possesses superior shield towards enzymes when the pore size matches the molecular size of enzymes [38]. Comparing with the above strategies, in-situ encapsulation, immobilizing enzyme within the MOFs framework structure during the preparation of MOFs, can also improve the stability thanks to rigid encapsulation of enzymes and perfectly avoids the pore size limitation of MOFs [36]. However, in-situ encapsulation still has to solve several problems such as synthetic conditions, biocompatibility, matching of enzymes and MOFs. Therefore, the design and fabrication of MOFs for enzyme immobilization using simple and
facile methods still remain a big challenge [39].
As one of the most common forms of vitamin ${\bf B}_{3}$ , nicotinamide (NAM) is an important industrial compound and always prepared by the hydrolysis of 3-cyanopyridine catalyzed by inorganic acid. Therefore, the production of NAM requires harsh reaction conditions and high-standard equipment, resulting in higher manufacturing costs [40-42]. In contrast, Nitrile hydratase (NHase, EC 4.2.1.84) can efficiently catalyze 3-cyanopyridine to nicotinamide through the hydration of nitriles under mild reaction conditions. NHase is a class of soluble metalloenzyme which can catalyze the hydration of nitrile to amide and widely used in the medicinal and food industries [40, 41, 43, 44]. However, the purification of NHase is always complex and expensive. Furthermore, the free NHase is too fragile to against extreme industrial environments, and thus it is quite essential to improve the stability and reusability through immobilization. In our previous researches, it has been proved that the addition of specific metal ions $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ) can increase the activity of NHase due to the important role of metal ions in its catalytic activity center [45]. Thus, in this research, the BioMOFs materials (Co-Cys nanoflowers) were prepared from cobalt salt and cystine as raw materials, meanwhile the NHase was in-situ encapsulated during the synthesis of BioMOFs [46-49]. The obtained NHase-BioMOFs biocomposits was named NHase $@$ Co-Cys. The preparation parameters of NHase@Co-Cys were optimized, and the stability of NHase $@$ Co-Cys was also investigated. The obtained NHase@Co-Cys were used as catalyst in synthesis of nicotinamide, and the catalytic performance was investigated in order to provide the basis for the future applications of this novel biocatalyst in industrial reactors such as tank reactor, tubular reactor and fixed-bed reactor.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
NHase ES-NHT-118 (EC 4.2.1.84, cobalt-type nitrile hydratase, $0.4\mathrm{mg}$ of protein per mL) from E. coli strain was purchased from Hangzhou Biosci Biotech Co., Ltd (China). Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd (China). L-cystine was purchased from Shanghai Lanji Technology Development Co., Ltd (China). Trypsin (bovine pancreas) was purchased from Shanghai Jinsui Biotechnology Co., Ltd (China). All other reagents were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2 Characterizations
SEM images were recorded on a FEI NanoSEM450 microscope. TEM images of the products were obtained using a JEOL 2100F transmission electron microscope. XPS spectra were recorded on a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha X-ray photoelectron spectrometer. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) images were taken with a Leica TCS SP5 confocal microscope with excitation wavelength of $488~\mathrm{nm}$ and emission wavelength of $525\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.3 Synthesis of NHase@Co-Cys
In a typical procedure, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of L-cystine solution (50 mM, pH 12.0) and $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution $(100\mathrm{mM})$ ) were added into $4.7\mathrm{mL}$ of Tris-HCl buffer $(10\ \mathrm{mM},\ \mathrm{pH}\ 8.0)$ containing NHase in various protein concentrations ranging from $0.05{\cdot}0.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . After incubation at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different times ranging from 1 to $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , the products were collected and washed with deionized water for three times. Finally, the immobilized NHase (refer to NHase $@$ Co-Cys) was obtained. The concentration of enzyme protein was determined through Bradford assay method. Typically, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of
NHase solution was mixed with $5\mathrm{\mL}$ of Bradford reagent and then reacted for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ . The absorbance was measured at $595\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The final NHase loading on NHase $@$ Co-Cys was the difference value between the amount of initial NHase and the amount of residual NHase in the supernatant and washing solutions.
2.4 Enzyme Activity Assay.
The activity of different preparation of NHase was assayed as previously reported [45]. Typically, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of acrylonitrile solution $(250\ \mathrm{mM}$ , pH 7.0) was mixed with $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of free or immobilized NHase solution $\mathrm{(pH}7.0)$ containing different amount of protein under stirring $(200\mathrm{rpm})$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{min}$ . Then $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of NaOH solution (2 M) was added to stop the reaction. The amount of produced acrylamide was determined by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) with an Eclipse Plus C18 column. One unit of activity (U) was defined as the amount of NHase that catalyzed acrylonitrile converting to 1 μmol acrylamide per minute. Relative activity was a percentage, representing the ratio of observed activity to the initial activity (the maximum activity).
## 2.5 The properties of free and immobilized NHase
2.5.1 Effect of temperature and pH on the activity of NHase
The free or immobilized NHase was incubated at different temperatures ranging from 20 to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in phosphate buffer $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , pH 7.0) or in different pH ranging from 4.0-10.0 (PBS, $50\mathrm{mM},$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then then their activities were measured respectively.
## 2.5.2 Thermal and $\mathrm{pH}$ stability of NHase
The free or immobilized NHase was incubated in PBS (50 mM, pH 7.0) at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different time ranging from 0 to $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and then the activity was measured to determine the thermal
stability. Similarly, in order to determine the $\mathsf{p H}$ stability, free and immobilized NHase were immersed in different buffer $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}2.0$ and 10.0) at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different time, then their residual activities were measured respectively.
## 2.5.3 Anti-proteolytic stability of NHase
Trypsin was selected as the model protease for the degradation of NHase to investigate its anti-proteolytic stability. The free and immobilized NHase were incubated in a certain amount of trypsin solution $\mathrm{(pH7.0,2mg/mL)}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different time $(0\mathrm{{h}},0.5\mathrm{{h}},1\mathrm{{h}})$ respectively, then their activities were measured respectively.
## 2.5.4 Mechanical stability of NHase
The free and immobilized NHase were incubated in phosphate buffer solution (50 mM, pH 7.0) for different time (from 0 to $8\mathrm{~h~}$ ) under different magnetic stirring conditions (100 rpm, 200 rpm, $300\ \mathrm{rpm})$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . To exclude the influence of temperature, free and immobilized NHase were incubated in phosphate buffer solution $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , pH 7.0) at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different time without stirring, as the control group. Then their residual activities were measured.
## 2.5.5 Storage stability of NHase
To determine the storage stability, both free and immobilized NHase were incubated in phosphate buffer solution $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , pH 7.0) and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in refrigerator for several days. Then the activity of each sample was measured every three days. The initial residual activity was considered as $100\%$ .
## 2.6 Effect of enzyme activity and reaction time on yield of nicotinamide
$0.5\mathrm{\ml}$ phosphate buffer that contained 3.63-14.21 U of free NHase or 3.18-12.76 U of immobilized NHase was added to the 5 ml 3-cyanopyridine $25\mathrm{mM},$ . The reaction was conducted at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ under shaking condition $(200~\mathrm{rpm})$ for different time. After that, the samples were taken out and the concentration of NAM was determined by HPLC, and the test conditions were the same as the methods described above.
## 2.7 Reusability of immobilized NHase on yield of nicotinamide
The reactions of producing nicotinamide that catalyzed by immobilized NHase were repeated for 10 cycles. After each cycle, immobilized NHase was centrifugally separated and washed three times with PBS (50 mM, pH 7.0), and then reintroduced into fresh reaction medium to investigate the reusability. The initial yield was considered to be $100\%$ .
## 2.8 Determination the kinetic parameters of NHase
The kinetic parameters of free NHase and immobilized NHase were obtained on the basis of Lineweaver-Burk plot. The immobilized NHase and free NHase were mixed with different concentration of acrylonitrile ranging from 2 to $250~\mathrm{mM}$ . The reaction was lasted for $5~\mathrm{min}$ . The production of acrylamide was measured by HPLC and calculated according to the standard curve.
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Characterization of Co-Cys BioMOFs and NHase@Co-Cys
As shown in Fig. 1a, the immobilized NHase (NHase@Co-Cys) was obtained from the in-situ encapsulation of NHase during the synthesis of Co-Cys BioMOFs. The morphology of Co-Cys BioMOFs and NHase $@$ Co-Cys were investigated by FE-SEM analysis (Fig. 1b and Fig. 1c). Fig. 1b showed that the Co-Cys BioMOFs were composed of uniform wrinkle and flower-like particles with a relative mean diameter of $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Compared to pure Co-Cys BioMOFs, the morphology of NHase $@$ Co-Cys (Fig. 1c) showed similar flower-like structure and diameter. Meanwhile, the surface of NHase $@$ Co-Cys was denser and the wrinkle becomes less obvious, which might be caused by in-situ encapsulation of NHase [50]. Because the NHase molecules could increase the local concentration of metal cation ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and organic ligands and then accelerated the synthesis of Co-Cys BioMOFs [19]. TEM image (Fig. 2) confirmed the uniform wrinkle flower-like structure of Co-Cys nanoparticles. The lighter colored area around the Co-Cys BioMOFs might be the gaps formed between adjacent nanosheets, and the edges of the Co-Cys BioMOFs were composed of nanosheets [13]. The TEM results were consistent with SEM results.
The successful immobilization of NHase was confirmed by CLSM analysis of NHase $@$ Co-Cys using FITC labeled NHase (Fig. 3). Compared with the bright field, the green color confirmed the successful immobilization of NHase [51]. What’s more, as shown in Fig. 3, it was obvious that NHase was uniformly distributed in the whole particle of the NHase@Co-Cys, which indicated the successful encapsulation of NHase during the BioMOF synthesis process.
The elemental composition and chemical state of Co-Cys BioMOFs were further investigated by XPS. The signals of Co, S, O, N and C elements could be found in the survey spectrum (Fig. 4a) [13, 7]. Fig. 4b-d showed the comparison data of the XPS N 1s, Co 2p and S 2p spectra. The Co 2p spectrum clearly showed two sharp peaks at $780.73\mathrm{eV}$ and $796.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ including two satellite peaks at $786.18\mathrm{~eV}$ and $802.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which was associated with the Co 2p3/2 and 2p1/2 characteristic spectrum of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ oxidation state, respectively. Meanwhile, the S 2p spectrum clearly showed a peak at $163.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which was assigned to $\mathrm{S}^{4+}$ oxidation state. These results further demonstrated the successful synthesis of Co-Cys BioMOFs.
## 3.2. Optimization of NHase@Co-Cys
In this work, NHase was immobilized through a simple in-situ encapsulation during the synthesis of Co-Cys BioMOFs. In the synthesis of NHase@Co-Cys, different incubation time exhibited influence on the specific activity of NHase $@$ Co-Cys and the NHase loading amount. As shown in Fig. 5, with the increase of incubation time, more and more enzyme molecules were encapsulated into the Co-Cys BioMOFs and the corresponding specific activity of the immobilized NHase increased firstly. A maximum specific activity of $139.04\mathrm{U/g_{im}}$ mobilized enzyme was achieved for an incubation time of 3 h. Meanwhile, the corresponding NHase loading amount was 95.85 mg/gimmobilized enzyme, and the protein encapsulation rate was $92.71\%$ . After the incubation time exceeded $3\mathrm{~h~}$ , the NHase loading amount kept increasing. The in-situ encapsulation of NHase during the synthesis of BioMOFs and the surface texture of BioMOFs biocomposits leaded to the higher coupling yield and enzyme loading amount. The loading amount tended to be invariable when the incubation time exceeded 5 h and the NHase immobilization reached full loading as 112.35 mg/gimmobilized enzyme. But the excessive loading might lead to the enzyme intermolecular steric hindrance and correspondingly increase the mass transfer resistance between the enzyme and substrate, resulting in a decline of specific activity [52]. Thus, the incubation time of $3\mathrm{{h}}$ was selected in subsequent experiments.
Fig. 6 (a) Effect of concentration of enzyme on specific activity of NHase $@$ Co-Cys. Effect of temperature (b) and $\mathrm{pH}$ (c) on activity of free NHase and NHase $@$ Co-Cys.
The specific activity of NHase $@$ Co-Cys increased gradually from 12.32 to $139.04\mathrm{~U}/\mathrm{g}\mathrm{;}$ mmobilized NHase with the increase of the enzyme concentration from 0.2 to $3.0~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and then decreased when the enzyme concentration was $4.2~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ (Fig. 6a). Proper concentration increase was beneficial to enzyme loading and immobilized enzyme specific activity. However, excessive enzyme concentration might lead to the local enrichment of enzyme molecules inside the immobilized enzyme, resulting in mutual cover-up of enzyme molecules [53]. What’s more, excessive enzyme molecules could cause difficulty in mass transfer between substrate and enzyme activity sites. All these factors always decrease the activity of NHase@Co-Cys. Thus, the incubation time of $3\mathrm{~h~}$ and the NHase concentration of $3\mathrm{\mg/mL}$ were selected as optimal conditions in the subsequent reactions.
## 3.3. Effect of temperature and pH on the activity of NHase
Temperature has a significant influence on reaction rate and the conformation of NHase molecule. The activity profiles of the free and immobilized NHase at different temperature values were shown in Fig. 6b. The relative activity of the free and immobilized NHase showed the same variation tendency, and the maximum activity for free NHase and NHase $@$ Co-Cys were achieved at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. What’s more, the NHase $@$ Co-Cys retained higher residual activity over the relatively high temperature range of $40{-}60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ than free NHase. Proper temperature could accelerate the molecular motion and increase the reaction rate. When the reaction temperature exceeded the optimum temperature, the inactivation process increased faster than the rate of catalysis as the temperature increased. However, excessive temperature might directly destroy the natural conformation of the enzyme molecule, causing degeneration and irreversible inactivation [54]. The shift of optimal temperature and the wider temperature range could be ascribed to the unique structure of the NHase@Co-Cys, which tightly bound the enzyme molecules in a certain space [55]. The structural properties needed higher temperature to stimulate the catalytic performance of enzyme molecules which could achieve the optimal conformation [56]. Moreover, the inactivation of NHase $@$ Co-Cys may follow different conformational changes depending on the degree of enzyme rigidification and inactivation conditions. In this study, NHase was in-situ encapsulated into the Co-Cys crystals during the synthesis of BioMOFs, and the fixed and rigid structure reduced the extension or folding of the peptide chains avoiding the denaturation and inactivation caused by temperature rise [57].
Free and immobilized NHase showed their optimal activity at $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ and $\mathrm{pH}6.0$ , respectively (Fig. 6c). Compared to free NHase, the optimal pH for NHase $@$ Co-Cys was found to be in the acidic region, which was ascribed to the electrostatic interaction between the matrix and solution, thus resulting in the unequal partitioning of $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ and OH in microenvironment and bulk solution [58, 59]. The NHase $@$ Co-Cys reserved higher residual activity than free NHase over the partial acid conditions ranging from pH 4 to 6. The free enzyme may experience protein aggregation (mainly near to the isoelectric point). As an acidic enzyme, the isoelectric point of NHase was $\mathrm{pH}3.6$ to 5.7 [57]. Compared with immobilized NHase, lower pH was more likely to lead to protein aggregation of free NHase, which may further affect the spatial structure of the enzyme and reduce its activity. Meanwhile, NHase $@$ Co-Cys retained higher residual activity over the broad pH range of 4.0 to 10.0, which was thanks to the crystal structure of BioMOFs acting as a “hard” frame to against the pH change in the microenvironment [60, 61].
## 3.4. Thermal and pH stability
After the successful immobilization of NHase on Co-Cys BioMOFs, the thermal and pH stability of NHase $@$ Co-Cys were investigated. As shown in Fig. 7a, after incubating free and immobilized NHase at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ , NHase $@$ Co-Cys retained $88.64\%$ and $72.56\%$ of the initial activities respectively, while for free NHase, only $28.37\%$ and $14.36\%$ of the initial activity was preserved. The half-lives $\left(\tau_{1/2}\right)$ of NHase $@$ Co-Cys at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were $5.3\mathrm{~h~}$ and $2.6\mathrm{~h~}$ , respectively, which were much higher than that of free NHase at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, free NHase was completely denatured and inactivated at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\ \mathrm{h}.$ while NHase $@$ Co-Cys still retained $52.91\%$ of the initial activity. After incubating at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ , NHase $@$ Co-Cys still retained high enzymatic activity and demonstrated excellent thermal stability. During the synthesis of BioMOFs, the NHase molecules were encapsulated into the crystal frameworks and the spatial restriction of enzyme molecular structure increased the endurance to excessive temperature [59, 62, 63]. In addition, ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions in the microenvironment could obviously increase the enzymatic activity of Co-type NHase and correspondingly improve the thermal stability of NHase $@$ Co-Cys [61].
As can be seen from Fig. 7b, after incubating at $\mathrm{pH}~4.0$ and 10.0 for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , NHase $@$ Co-Cys retained $83.96\%$ and $78.67\%$ of their maximum activities respectively, while for free NHase, only $40.35\%$ and $19.25\%$ were retained, respectively. NHase $@$ Co-Cys also showed high stability under acidic conditions and alkaline conditions $\mathrm{\Phi_{\mathrm{pH}4.0}}$ and 10.0, respectively) after incubating for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ , and more than $58.62\%$ of its initial activity was retained. In comparison, free NHase was almost completely inactivated whether at $\mathrm{pH}4.0$ or 10.0 after incubating for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The increased stability of NHase $@$ Co-Cys in response to $\mathrm{pH}$ was associated with the suitable microenvironment adapted by the immobilization process [56, 64].
## 3.5. Anti-proteolytic and mechanical stability
The anti-proteolytic stability of NHase@Co-Cys and free NHase was investigated and the results were presented in Fig. 8a. After incubated in the trypsin solution for 1h, $44.27\%$ and $70.14\%$ of the original activity were retained for free NHase and NHase $@$ Co-Cys, respectively. The tolerance of NHase@Co-cys to protease hydrolysis was enhanced, which was because of the “coat” of Co-Cys [46, 65]. As expected, the robust “coat” could also improve the mechanical stability of NHase $@$ Co-Cys under vigorous stirring conditions (100 rpm, 200 rpm, 300 rpm), which was demonstrated in Fig. 8b. With the extension of incubation time and the speed of magnetic stirring, the activity of free enzyme decreased significantly. In contrast, NHase@Co-Cys retained higher relative activity. Under the magnetic stirring condition of $300\ \mathrm{rpm}$ , NHase $@$ Co-cys remained $96.72\%$ of the initial activity after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $89.35\%$ after 8h. However, free enzyme only remained $45.47\%$ and $18.32\%$ of the initial activity after 4h and 8h, respectively. Compared to other reported immobilized NHase [66], NHase@Co-Cys also exhibited improved mechanical stability $(89.35\%)$ under the same conditions as $300\mathrm{rpm}$ after 8h.
## 3.6. Storage stability
The activity of free enzyme decreased seriously and retained $63.96\%$ of the initial activity during the initial 12 days, and only retained $33.91\%$ of the initial activity after 27 days (Fig. 8c). In comparison, the NHase $@$ Co-Cys still retained $85.39\%$ and $69.61\%$ of the initial activity after 12 and 27 days, respectively. This suggested that the Co-Cys bio-MOFs tightly entrapped the NHase molecules to keep the active conformation, and further improved the reaction microenvironment to avoid the deactivation of enzyme [67]. The decrease of the NHase $@$ Co-Cys activity was mainly due to the enzyme detachment from the support and the formation of multiple covalent linkages between NHase molecules in long term storage. In addition, the change of surrounding environment and the microbial degradation might also lead to the loss of enzyme activity.
## 3.7. Preparation of nicotinamide by free NHase and NHase@Co-Cys
As an important industrial enzyme, NHase has been successfully applied in lots of industry production. Nicotinamide, an important industrial compound, was used as a model product to investigate the catalytic performance of NHase@Co-Cys and free NHase. The effects of activity and reaction time on the yield of nicotinamide were measured and shown in Fig. 9a and Fig. 9b. Free NHase and NHase $@$ Co-Cys showed the same trends with the change of enzyme amount and reaction time. The maximum yield of niacinamide reached $90.94\%$ and $86.36\%$ after $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ by free NHase (14.51 U) and NHase $@$ Co-Cys (12.76 U), respectively. These results indicated that the NHase $@$ Co-Cys showed similar catalytic capacity compared with free NHase on producing nicotinamide. Furthermore, compared with other immobilized NHase, such as cross-linked enzyme aggregates (CLEAs) [68] and poly(vinylalcohol) (PVA)/chitosan complex [69], the NHase $@$ Co-Cys provided better protection for enzyme molecules and showed better activity. Benefiting from the immobilization, the immobilized enzyme could effectively reduce the substrate inhibition with excellent stability and reusability. The NHase $@$ Co-Cys could be extended to the production of other high value amides such as vitamin nicotinamide and demonstrated a broad application prospect in food and pharmaceutical industries [70, 71].
